theory.intro:
A **rectilinear figure** is made only of straight sides with right-angle turns at its corners. Its interior angles can be $90^\circ$ or $270^\circ$. The figure can be rotated so its sides are horizontal or vertical. When you trace its boundary in one direction, what runs one way must total the same as what runs back the other way. That balance finds an unlabelled side.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (6,0) node[midway, below] {$a$};
\draw (6,0) -- (6,2) node[midway, right] {$q$};
\draw (6,2) -- (3,2) node[midway, above] {$c$};
\draw (3,2) -- (3,5) node[midway, right] {$r$};
\draw (3,5) -- (0,5) node[midway, above] {$b$};
\draw (0,5) -- (0,0) node[midway, left] {$p$};
\node at (3,-1.1) {$a = b + c$};
\node at (3,-1.9) {$p = q + r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
Group sides by the direction you travel along them when tracing the boundary, rather than by where they sit on the page. In this diagram, start at the bottom-left corner and trace along the bottom to the right: $q$ and $t$ run up, while $s$ and $p$ run down.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (6,0);
\draw (6,0) -- (6,3) node[midway, right] {$q$};
\draw (6,3) -- (4,3);
\draw (4,3) -- (4,1) node[midway, left] {$s$};
\draw (4,1) -- (2,1);
\draw (2,1) -- (2,5) node[midway, right] {$t$};
\draw (2,5) -- (0,5);
\draw (0,5) -- (0,0) node[midway, left] {$p$};
\node at (3,-1.2) {$q + t = p + s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

